{"id": "sevt_01DYFWSadsMnpfACxm8oDSJ6", "content": [{"text": "**Evidence**\n- **Deploys:** The checkout deploy `7bd6579` (\"simplify order summary assembly\") went out at 09:27:31. The auth deploy at 08:52 and the cart deploy at 05:41 are too early to line up.\n- **Checkout p99:** It was steady at about 75 ms until 09:26. At 09:27 it jumped to 3.5 s, and by 09:50 it was about 5 s. The gap from deploy to spike is under a minute.\n- **Checkout error rate:** It was 0 until 09:35. It then rose to roughly 0.2\u20130.5 from 09:36, which fits timeouts building up behind the slow requests.\n- **Diff:** `CheckoutSummaryHandler.build_summary` used to run one `order_id IN (...)` query and group the items in memory. The new code runs `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` once for every open order, which is an N+1 pattern.\n- **Log:** From 09:27:32 on, there are 3,486 per-order queries across 293 requests, about 12 per request and up to 16. Before the deploy, the log shows only the batched `order_id IN (?)` form.\n- **Unrelated noise:** The GC pauses and the auth identity-provider timeout in the log are separate from this spike.\n\nI didn't check `db_pool_utilization`. The runbook only calls for it when no deploy lines up, and one did.\n\n**Suggested fix:** Revert `7bd6579`, or restore the batched query and keep the clearer loop for the summarising step.\n\n> **Root cause:** `7bd6579` \u2014 `build_summary` replaced the single batched `order_items` query with one query per open order, so each checkout summary now makes about 12 sequential DB round-trips and p99 went from about 75 ms to several seconds.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T06:01:40.215784Z", "type": "agent.message"}
